message: "If you use this benchmark, code, or experimental findings, please cite it as below."
authors:
  - family-names: "Research Software Engineering Team"
    given-names: "Senior ML Researchers"
title: "Candidate Order Instability in Non-Autoregressive Multi-Candidate Transformers"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-09-23"
license: "Apache-2.0"
repository-code: "https://github.com/anshull-saxena/candidate-order-instability"
abstract: >-
  Non-autoregressive transformer architectures evaluate multi-candidate decisions by concatenating
  candidate-specific markers into a single sequence processed by a bidirectional encoder. We demonstrate
  that candidate presentation order induces substantial decision instability that scales superlinearly
  with candidate cardinality K (Argmax flip rate: 5.70% at K=5 to 47.28% at K=77). While canonical
  alphabetical sorting produces deterministic repeatability, counterfactual reverse sorting reveals a
  55.83% cross-order disagreement rate at K=77. We analyze random and orthogonal cyclic marginalization
  as inference-time interventions, showing that cyclic shifts suppress residual instability to 16.11%
  at M=5 without requiring architectural retraining.
keywords:
  - transformer evaluation
  - non-autoregressive decision models
  - order sensitivity
  - permutation marginalization
  - ranking systems
  - calibration
